Consider a discrete-time random graph process $(G_n)_{n\ge 0}$ that generalizes linear preferential attachment by allowing both growth and removal. At each step $n\to n+1$, with prescribed probabilities one of the following operations is performed on $G_n$:
(i) a new vertex is added and connected by one (or finitely many) edges to existing vertices, each existing vertex $v$ being chosen with probability proportional to $\deg_{G_n}(v) + a$ for some fixed parameter $a > -1$;
(ii) a new edge is added between two existing vertices, again chosen with probability proportional to (degree + $a$);
(iii) an existing edge is deleted according to some specified rule (e.g.\ uniformly at random, or with probability depending on the degrees of its endpoints);
(iv) an existing vertex (together with its incident edges) is deleted according to some specified rule (e.g.\ uniformly, by age, or with probability depending on degree).

Fix such a removal-augmented preferential attachment dynamics. Investigate the following questions:

(a) (Giant component.) For which choices of the growth/removal probabilities and removal rules does $G_n$ contain, for all sufficiently large $n$, a connected component of size $\Theta(|V(G_n)|)$ with high probability? Determine the phase diagram in the parameters separating regimes with a giant component from regimes in which all components are of sublinear order.

(b) (Persistence of the maximum degree.) Let $M_n \in V(G_n)$ denote a vertex of maximum degree in $G_n$ (breaking ties by some fixed rule). Determine whether the maximum-degree vertex is persistent, i.e.\ whether there exists a (random) vertex $v^\star$ and a finite (random) time $N$ such that $M_n = v^\star$ for all $n \ge N$ almost surely, or whether instead $M_n$ changes infinitely often.

(c) More generally, describe how the chosen removal mechanism affects global structural quantities of $G_n$ beyond the expected degree sequence, including component sizes, diameter, and the trajectory of the maximum-degree vertex.

